the decision to end the sci-fi cult  favorite after two seasons has already  been made but why exactly did Netflix  execs decide to pull the plug on their  mind-bending mystery thriller here are a  few reasons why we think the oay was  cancelled Netflix doesn't release its  viewing figures so it's impossible to  say whether the amount of people  streaming the oay  impacted the decision to accent what we  do know is that the streamer makes these  decisions based on a simple ratio  viewership versus cost Netflix has  confirmed that it adheres to this  formula and according to The Wrap an  Insider with the knowledge of Netflix's  decision to cancel the drama two seasons  in has confirmed that the oay fell on  the wrong side of the equation the oay  was a big creative swing for the company  the source set and while it was one that  Netflix was proud to take it just wasn't  paying off in June 2019 Netflix chief  content officer Ted Saran dose  reportedly ordered dozens of his TV and  movie execs to rein in their spending  people familiar with the high level  meeting told the information it was made  clear that bigger budget projects needed  to bring in the viewers going forward  building buzz and Industry credibility  was no longer enough the Netflix  business model relies not only on the  retention of subscribers but the steady  influx of new ones and shows like the Oh  a don't help in that department  it was critically acclaimed yes but the  Netflix metric deem that it was no  longer a draw as a report by the  information put it Netflix has learned  that the first two seasons of a show are  key to bringing in subscribers but the  third in later seasons don't do much to  retain or win new subscribers and being  a show after the second season saves  money because show runners who oversee  production tend to negotiate a boost and  pay after two years understandably this  approach isn't popular with producers  especially those running shows designed  to be told over multiple seasons like  the oa1 creator of a Netflix show that  was axed after two seasons alleged it's  really hard to get Netflix to put money  into a third season for a creator you  want to be able to reward your audience  with more seasons she's gotta have it  American vandal and friends from college  all cancelled in 2019 also only got two  seasons Netflix has denied that this is  a new policy on the surface the oay is  your typical binge Worthy Netflix show  teasing viewers with glimpses of what's  to come in order to keep them rooted in  their seats for hours on  and that relationship between show and  viewer only functions if the build-up to  those glimpses is satisfying enough in  its own right however and in the case of  the Oh a that was very much a one-sided  relationship simply put the Oh a  required viewers to sit through hours of  speculative twists and turns for  ultimately buried little payoff in  return questions were answered with  questions that led to yet more questions  while traditional TV networks may be  willing to churn out  endless episodes of a show based on  mystery alone much like ABC's lost  Netflix isn't going to continue to fund  a show that loses people's interest  before they get to the payoff the oay  isn't for everybody  it's wildly imaginative and incredibly  well-executed but its mysteries are  often a bit too impenetrable part 1  ended with a school shooting dwara sake  of clarity what we'll call interpretive  dance one character swallows a sea  anenome during a near-death experience  in the same episode in which another  character swallows a bird given to her  by an angel with Braille on her face the  Oh a part two is equally weird and for  some critics it just went too far  according to indie wires Ben Travers  part 2 doubles down in a longer more  pretentious and less vigorous follow-up  season that's part puzzlebox part  romance and wholly insane if there's one  single moment in season 2 of the Oh a  that seemed to push people over the edge  it's when Britt Marlins original angel  meets a telepathic octopus yes a  telepathic octopus we had already been  through a whole lot of craziness by the  time the show's protagonists encounters  this creature whose name by the way is  old night I hold hope for your species  but he holds hope for our species this  extraordinary octopus communicates with  revelers at a packed nightclub via our  dimension traversing hero  and it was simply too much for some  viewers even ardent fans of the show  were left uh turley perplexed by what  they had witnessed and in true Oh a  fashion answers weren't exactly  forthcoming is this I just - lights bro  one of the biggest issues with the oay  is that it seemed to be catering almost  exclusively to a cult audience the show  built up a dedicated following after its  first season and rather than trying to  expand that following by making season 2  a little more accessible to new viewers  the oay seem to lean even further into  the weirdness the result was the second  season that isolated anyone that didn't  have the patience for telepathic  octopuses and dancing robots and  isolating potential customers isn't part  of the Netflix business model good the  fact that the oay pandered to expand I'm  a little too much have been a factor in  Netflix pulling the plug  according to slash films Joshua Meyer  the show seemed to take pleasure in the  fact that it created an exclusive club  for people who were willing to go along  with whatever it threw at them  Meyer wrote in his review of the O a  part 2 it gathers fans around itself in  a candlelit attic and seeks to regale  them with the tall tale the show has big  ideas but its characters are all too  quick to chase magic mirrors on blind  faith if you're invested in those  characters then you might be willing to  drink from the same pitcher of kool-aid  that they do could co-creators of the O  a Brit Marling and saw Batman Cleese  already be working on another project  for N